    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-113604",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9c976b6a-4523-51e1-a243-27aa8937db25",
      "knowledge_point": "Expressing the Relationship Between the Rows of Pascal's Triangle in Terms of Binomial Coefficients",
      "problem": "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2}{1})=(\\frac{1}{0})+[]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "To find $(\\frac{2}{1}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $2$ and position $1$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\frac{2}{1})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\frac{2}{1})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2}{1})=(\\frac{1}{0})+(\\frac{1}{1})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\frac{1}{1}).$",
